## 3.2 Module B: Automatic photo organisation

This is the first major intelligence layer.

The system should organise photographs using multiple dimensions rather than relying on a single criterion.

### Organisation hierarchy

I recommend:

```text
PERSONAL PHOTO LIBRARY
       |
       +-- YEAR
       |    |
       |    +-- 2020
       |    |    +-- January
       |    |    +-- February
       |    |    +-- March
       |    |
       |    +-- 2021
       |    +-- 2022
       |    +-- 2023
       |
       +-- LOCATIONS
       |    +-- United Kingdom
       |    +-- Nigeria
       |    +-- France
       |
       +-- EVENTS
       |    +-- Birthdays
       |    +-- Holidays
       |    +-- Family gatherings
       |    +-- Graduations
       |
       +-- PEOPLE (OPT-IN)
       |
       +-- THEMES
            +-- Nature
            +-- Food
            +-- Architecture
            +-- Beaches
            +-- Celebrations
```

The important distinction is that these should be overlapping views of the same photographs, not separate physical copies.

A photograph could simultaneously belong to:

- August 2022.
- London.
- Family holiday.
- Birthday celebration.
- A particular person's collection.

### Event clustering

This is particularly valuable.

The system should be able to identify groups of photographs likely to represent the same event.

Possible signals include:

| Signal | Example |
|---|---|
| Time proximity | Photographs taken within 30 minutes |
| Date proximity | Photographs taken over several consecutive days |
| Location similarity | Photographs taken within a particular geographical radius |
| Visual similarity | Similar environments, backgrounds and subjects |
| Metadata | Camera timestamps and GPS coordinates |
| Image content | Beach, restaurant, airport, birthday cake |
| Sequence | Photographs taken in a natural chronological sequence |

A clustering algorithm could combine these signals to identify likely events.

For example:

- 10:00 – Airport.
- 13:30 – Aircraft.
- 17:00 – Hotel.
- Following day – Beach.
- Following day – Restaurant.

The system might suggest:

**Possible event: Family holiday in Spain**

However, the platform should present this as a suggestion, not a verified fact.

The user can confirm the album and provide its actual name.

### A particularly useful feature: confidence-based organisation

I would introduce confidence levels.

| Confidence | Interpretation | Behaviour |
|---|---|---|
| High | Strong metadata and temporal evidence | Automatically organise |
| Medium | Probable event or location | Suggest for confirmation |
| Low | Insufficient evidence | Leave unclassified |

This prevents the system from presenting uncertain interpretations as established personal history.

