## 3.3 Module C: AI-generated memory captions

I consider this one of the strongest potential differentiators.

However, the quality of the captions will matter more than the sophistication of the underlying AI.

Compare the following.

**Basic caption:**

"A group of people standing outside."

**Better caption:**

"A group of people standing together outside a building on a sunny day."

**Context-enriched caption:**

"Several people gathered outside a building during a daytime occasion. The photograph was taken on 14 June 2022 in Birmingham."

The third caption is more useful because it combines:

- Visual information.
- Verified date metadata.
- Available geographical information.

But it should not claim that the people are family members, colleagues or friends unless that information has been provided or explicitly confirmed.

### Recommended caption-generation architecture

Each photograph should have three distinct information categories.

| Information category | Example | Source |
|---|---|---|
| Verified metadata | 14 June 2022, 15:32 | EXIF |
| Visual description | People standing outside a building | Computer vision |
| Contextual interpretation | Possible graduation celebration | AI inference |

These categories should be maintained separately in the database.

I would also store:

- Generated caption.
- Confidence or uncertainty information.
- Model used.
- Generation date.
- User-edited caption.
- User confirmation status.

### Caption personalisation

The platform should allow three styles:

1. **Simple:** Short factual descriptions.
2. **Descriptive:** More detailed descriptions of the photograph.
3. **Memory style:** Natural, engaging descriptions combining verified metadata and visual observations.

For example:

> A beautiful summer afternoon in London. The photograph captures a group enjoying time together in an outdoor setting.

The system should not turn an ordinary photograph into a fabricated personal story.

### Album descriptions

This is even more interesting.

Suppose an album contains 120 photographs from a holiday.

The system can generate:

**Album: Summer Holiday – August 2022**

"Explore this collection of photographs taken during August 2022. The album includes outdoor scenery, sightseeing, meals and photographs captured across several locations."

If the locations are verified, the system can incorporate them.

The user can edit the resulting description.

---

## 3.4 Module D: Automatic album generation

This should be a central product feature.

I recommend supporting two types of albums.

**Type 1: Rule-based albums**

Automatically created using reliable metadata.

Examples:
- 2020 photographs.
- July 2021.
- Photographs taken in London.
- All photographs from a particular date.

These are relatively straightforward to implement.

**Type 2: AI-assisted event albums**

Created using multiple signals.

Examples:
- Family holiday.
- Birthday celebration.
- Graduation.
- Christmas gathering.
- Wedding.
- Weekend trip.

These require more sophisticated analysis.

### Proposed album-generation workflow

```text
ALL IMPORTED PHOTOGRAPHS
          |
          v
     TEMPORAL GROUPING
          |
          v
    LOCATION ANALYSIS
          |
          v
   VISUAL SIMILARITY
          |
          v
     EVENT CLUSTERING
          |
          v
     AI INTERPRETATION
          |
          v
   SUGGESTED ALBUMS
          |
          v
     USER REVIEW
          |
          v
    APPROVED ALBUMS
```

The user should see something like:

**We found 12 possible albums in your photographs.**

- Summer holiday – 86 photographs.
- Christmas 2021 – 42 photographs.
- Birthday celebration – 31 photographs.
- London visit – 54 photographs.
- Family gathering – 27 photographs.

Each suggestion should offer:

- View photographs.
- Accept album.
- Rename album.
- Remove photographs.
- Merge albums.
- Split albums.
- Edit description.

The system should learn from user corrections, but only within clearly defined privacy and consent boundaries.

