AI processing can take time and should be treated as a background activity.

The user should immediately see:
- Uploaded photographs.
- Available dates.
- Basic chronological organisation.
- Processing progress.

More sophisticated captions and event suggestions can appear progressively.

This creates a much better first impression.

# 5. AI and technical architecture

This is where I would be particularly careful about controlling complexity.

The platform does not need a large language model to perform every operation. In fact, using an LLM for everything would increase costs, slow down processing and make the product unnecessarily dependent on external AI providers.

I recommend a **hybrid AI architecture**, combining conventional software engineering, computer vision, machine learning and multimodal LLMs.

## 5.1 Which functions actually require AI?

| Functionality | Recommended technology | Requires AI? |
|---|---|---|
| Upload photographs | File upload service | No |
| Extract dates and camera information | EXIF metadata extraction | No |
| Chronological organisation | Database queries | No |
| Exact duplicate detection | Cryptographic hashing | No |
| Similar-image detection | Perceptual hashing | No |
| Location extraction | GPS metadata | No |
| Reverse geocoding | Geolocation API | No |
| Event clustering | Machine learning and rules | Partially |
| Image understanding | Computer vision | Yes |
| Semantic image search | Image embeddings | Yes |
| Natural-language search | LLM plus retrieval | Partially |
| Caption generation | Multimodal LLM | Yes |
| Album descriptions | LLM | Yes |
| Memory summaries | LLM plus structured data | Yes |
| Timeline generation | Database and visualisation | No |
| Facial recognition | Computer vision | Yes, optional |

This division is important because many of the most frequently executed functions can be performed without incurring AI inference costs.

## 5.2 Recommended technology components

### A. Metadata extraction

**Recommendation: ExifTool**

ExifTool is a mature utility for extracting metadata from photographs and other media files.

It supports:
- EXIF.
- IPTC.
- XMP.
- GPS coordinates.
- Camera information.
- Capture timestamps.
- Image orientation.

Official resource: [ExifTool documentation](https://exiftool.org/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

This should be one of the first components implemented.

A critical detail: metadata is not always reliable.

Some photographs:
- Have incorrect timestamps.
- Have no GPS information.
- Have been downloaded or edited.
- Have had metadata stripped.
- Have been copied between devices.

The system should therefore maintain both the original metadata and any user-corrected values.

### B. Image understanding and similarity

I recommend investigating CLIP-based image embeddings.

CLIP-style models create numerical representations of images and text in a shared semantic space.

This enables the platform to compare:
- Two photographs.
- A photograph and a text description.
- Different photographs containing related visual concepts.

For example, a user searching for "a sunset over the sea" can retrieve photographs based on semantic similarity, even if the filename contains no relevant words.

Relevant technologies:

| Technology | Application |
|---|---|
| OpenCLIP | Open-source image and text embeddings |
| Hugging Face Transformers | Model integration and inference |
| PyTorch | Model execution |
| FAISS | Similarity search |
| pgvector | Vector storage and retrieval within PostgreSQL |

Hugging Face provides documented image feature extraction and similarity workflows. [GitHub](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers/blob/main/docs/source/en/tasks/image_feature_extraction.md?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

My recommendation is to start with an established pretrained embedding model rather than training a new computer vision model.

### C. Multimodal LLMs

Use a multimodal model to generate captions and interpret more complicated visual contexts.

Potential providers include:

- OpenAI — [OpenAI API](https://platform.openai.com/docs/overview?utm_source=chatgpt.com)
- Google — [Gemini API](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs?utm_source=chatgpt.com)
- Anthropic — [Anthropic API](https://docs.anthropic.com/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Open-source alternatives could include vision-language models such as Qwen-VL and other models available through Hugging Face.

I would initially use a commercial API for caption generation because it reduces infrastructure and model-maintenance requirements.

However, the system should be designed with a provider-independent interface.

This means that changing from one AI provider to another should not require rebuilding the application.

OpenAI's documentation explains how image inputs are processed and how image dimensions and detail levels affect token consumption. This is important when estimating the cost of analysing thousands of photographs. [OpenAI Developers](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/images-vision?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

### D. Event clustering

I would not initially use an LLM to determine every event.

A better approach is:

1. Group photographs by time.
2. Calculate geographical proximity.
3. Identify visual similarity.
4. Examine changes in scene and subject.
5. Apply clustering algorithms.
6. Ask an LLM to generate a human-readable interpretation of sufficiently strong clusters.

Potential algorithms include:

- DBSCAN.
- HDBSCAN.
- Hierarchical clustering.
- K-means, where appropriate.

DBSCAN and HDBSCAN are particularly interesting because the number of events is not known in advance.

For example, the system might discover that 300 photographs represent six separate events.

The AI layer can then help describe those events.

### E. Facial recognition

I would deliberately postpone this functionality.

Facial recognition introduces:
- Additional privacy obligations.
- Greater technical complexity.
- Difficulties with photographs containing multiple people.
- Errors involving similar-looking individuals.
- Potential concerns involving children and family photographs.

Ente demonstrates that on-device facial recognition is a practical privacy-preserving approach. [ente](https://ente.photos/help/photos/features/search-and-discovery/face-recognition?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

For our product, I recommend:

- No facial identification by default.
- Explicit opt-in.
- On-device processing where practical.
- User-controlled naming.
- Ability to remove or correct groupings.
- No automatic identification of people through external identity databases.

The platform can still offer extensive photo organisation without facial recognition.

