## 5.3 Recommended application architecture

For the initial product, I would favour a modular monolith with asynchronous processing rather than microservices.

Microservices would introduce unnecessary operational complexity at this stage.

### Proposed architecture

```text
                 USERS
                   |
          +--------+--------+
          |                 |
        WEB APP          MOBILE APP
          |                 |
          +--------+--------+
                   |
              API GATEWAY
                   |
          APPLICATION BACKEND
                   |
       +-----------+-----------+
       |           |           |
   USER/AUTH    PHOTO       ALBUM
    SERVICE    SERVICE     SERVICE
       |           |           |
       +-----------+-----------+
                   |
             POSTGRESQL
                   |
       +-----------+-----------+
       |           |           |
   METADATA    EMBEDDINGS    EVENTS
    STORAGE     (VECTOR)    DATABASE
                   |
             TASK QUEUE
                   |
          BACKGROUND WORKERS
                   |
       +-----------+-----------+
       |           |           |
    METADATA    COMPUTER      AI
   EXTRACTION    VISION    CAPTIONING
       |           |           |
       +-----------+-----------+
                   |
              OBJECT STORAGE
                   |
           ORIGINAL PHOTOS
           THUMBNAILS
           PREVIEWS
```

The important principle is that the original photograph, its metadata, its AI-generated description and its album membership should be managed as distinct but related data.

### Recommended technology stack

| Component | Recommended technology | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Web frontend | Next.js / React | Mature ecosystem and responsive interface |
| Styling | Tailwind CSS | Efficient interface development |
| Mobile | Progressive Web App initially | Lower initial development cost |
| Native mobile | React Native later | Cross-platform expansion |
| Backend | Python / FastAPI | Excellent AI and image-processing ecosystem |
| Database | PostgreSQL | Reliable relational data management |
| Vector search | pgvector | Avoid separate vector database initially |
| Object storage | Cloudflare R2 | Cost-effective media storage |
| Authentication | Supabase Auth or equivalent | Faster implementation |
| Image processing | Pillow / libvips | Thumbnail generation and image transformations |
| Metadata | ExifTool | Comprehensive metadata extraction |
| AI processing | PyTorch / Hugging Face | Computer vision and embeddings |
| AI captions | Commercial multimodal API | Rapid MVP implementation |
| Task processing | Redis with Celery | Asynchronous image processing |
| Deployment | Cloudflare / managed cloud hosting | Reduced infrastructure management |

### Why Cloudflare R2?

Photographs can consume substantial storage.

Cloudflare R2 currently lists standard storage at $0.015 per GB-month, with no internet egress charges for its standard storage class. [Cloudflare Docs](https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/pricing/?ref=renewalrecord.com\&utm_source=chatgpt.com)

For illustration:

| Stored data | Approximate monthly storage cost |
|---|---:|
| 100GB | $1.50 |
| 500GB | $7.50 |
| 1TB | $15 |
| 5TB | $75 |
| 10TB | $150 |

These are storage charges only, excluding operations, processing, backups and other infrastructure.

For an early-stage business, this is attractive.

### Why PostgreSQL and Supabase?

Supabase provides managed PostgreSQL, authentication, storage and related services.

Its published Pro pricing starts at $25/month, with 100GB of file storage included in that plan. Additional storage is charged separately. [Supabase](https://supabase.com/pricing?trk=public_post_comment-text\&utm_source=chatgpt.com)

I would use PostgreSQL for:
- User accounts.
- Photo metadata.
- Album records.
- Event relationships.
- Search indexes.
- User preferences.
- AI processing status.

I would not store original photographs directly in PostgreSQL.

Store them in object storage and maintain references in the database.

