# 6. Privacy, security and user trust

I would treat privacy as a foundational product requirement.

People may upload:
- Photographs of children.
- Family gatherings.
- Private celebrations.
- Home interiors.
- Holiday locations.
- Personal documents accidentally included in collections.
- Photographs containing sensitive contextual information.

A data breach could seriously undermine the business.

## 6.1 Recommended privacy architecture

| Requirement | Recommended implementation |
|---|---|
| Data transmission | HTTPS/TLS |
| Stored photographs | Encryption at rest |
| Account security | Secure authentication and optional MFA |
| Access control | Strict user-level authorisation |
| Album sharing | Private by default |
| AI processing | Explicitly documented processing arrangements |
| Facial recognition | Disabled by default |
| Deletion | Permanent deletion workflows |
| Data portability | Bulk download and export |
| Account removal | Defined deletion and retention process |
| AI training | No use of personal photographs for model training |

### An important distinction: encrypted storage versus end-to-end encryption

These are not equivalent.

If photographs are encrypted at rest but decrypted on the server for AI processing, the service provider may technically be able to access their contents.

True end-to-end encryption introduces more complicated constraints because server-side AI cannot simply inspect encrypted images.

I would therefore consider two processing modes:

**Mode A: Standard private cloud processing**

- Encrypted storage.
- Secure server-side image processing.
- Restricted employee access.
- Contractual controls over AI providers.
- No training on user photographs.
- Clear disclosure of processing arrangements.

**Mode B: Privacy-enhanced processing**

- Local image analysis where practical.
- On-device embeddings and face processing.
- Minimal transmission to external AI providers.
- Optional user-controlled cloud AI.

Do not advertise the product as end-to-end encrypted unless the complete architecture genuinely supports that claim.

## 6.2 UK GDPR considerations

For a UK-based commercial service, the business should consider:

- UK GDPR.
- Data Protection Act 2018.
- Data Protection Act amendments and relevant changes introduced through the Data (Use and Access) Act 2025.
- Lawful basis for processing.
- Privacy notices.
- Data processor agreements.
- Data subject rights.
- International data transfers.
- Retention and deletion policies.

The ICO explains that facial photographs do not automatically constitute special-category biometric data. They become biometric data in the relevant legal sense when specific technical processing is used for unique identification. [ICO](https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/lawful-basis/special-category-data/what-is-special-category-data/?trk=article-ssr-frontend-pulse_little-text-block\&utm_source=chatgpt.com)

A Data Protection Impact Assessment should be undertaken before deploying potentially high-risk processing. The ICO specifically identifies innovative technology combined with other high-risk characteristics as a situation requiring careful assessment. [ICO](https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/accountability-and-governance/data-protection-impact-assessments-dpias/when-do-we-need-to-do-a-dpia/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

I would commission a DPIA before introducing large-scale facial identification or other sensitive profiling functionality.

International processing also matters. Using an overseas cloud or AI provider can create UK GDPR international-transfer obligations depending on the parties and arrangements involved. [ICO](https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/international-transfers/a-guide-to-international-transfers/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

### My recommended trust proposition

Make the following commitments part of the product:

1. Your photographs remain yours.
2. Your photographs are never publicly visible by default.
3. Your photographs are not used to train external AI models.
4. You decide whether facial recognition is enabled.
5. You can export your entire collection.
6. You can permanently delete your account and photographs.
7. AI-generated descriptions can be corrected or removed.

These commitments should be reflected in contracts, technical controls and operational procedures, not just promotional wording.

