# 19. The three decisions I would make before development begins

If I were leading this venture, I would want the founders to settle three strategic questions.

### Decision A: What exactly are we selling?

There are three possible directions:

| Direction | Product proposition | My assessment |
|---|---|---|
| A. Photo management platform | Organise and manage photographs | Highly competitive |
| B. AI-powered memory platform | Turn photographs into meaningful memories | Strongest differentiation |
| C. Digital memory preservation service | Organise and preserve personal photographic archives | Interesting commercial entry point |

**My recommendation: Combine B and C.**

Build a memory platform, initially targeting people who need help organising their existing photographic archives.

### Decision B: Who owns the storage?

Three options exist:

1. We store everything.
2. Users connect their own cloud storage.
3. We offer both options.

I recommend starting with controlled cloud storage, while designing the architecture to support external storage integrations later.

### Decision C: What is the first compelling customer experience?

I would focus the entire MVP around this:

> Upload 1,000 disorganised photographs. Within a reasonable time, receive a beautifully organised chronological collection, suggested event albums, meaningful captions and a searchable visual history.

If the product can deliver this reliably, it has something genuinely useful to sell.

